					<item>
				<guid>http://www.scpwiki.com/forum/t-9819650#post-4108790</guid>
				<title>(no title)</title>
				<link>http://www.scpwiki.com/forum/t-9819650/scp-1322-j#post-4108790</link>
				<description></description>
				<pubDate>Sat, 05 Jan 2019 01:00:51 +0000</pubDate>
				<wikidot:authorName>Mark Penrice</wikidot:authorName>				<wikidot:authorUserId>1858819</wikidot:authorUserId>				<content:encoded>
					<![CDATA[
						 <p>Hehe. I had it figured out a sentence or so before the testing logs started (after momentarily thinking it was something about the techs' own proteins that blocked them going through - and the more likely chiral problem you'd expect with an extradimensional break room would be indigestable food&#8230;), but I still chuckled and read to the end. As others have said, it's just the right level of dumb.</p> <p>Small point of order, though, &quot;60&#160;km&quot; isn't a speed. &quot;60&#160;km/h&quot; would be. Though the foundation would be more likely to use metres/sec than kilometres/hour for such testing; as 60&#160;m/s would be extremely fast for the kind of drone you might try to launch into a magic mirror, you'd want to correct that to somewhere between 10 and 20&#160;m/s (= about 36 to 72&#160;km/h, or 22.5 to 45 mph, much faster than it sounds when within a tight space like a company canteen and pushing the limits for domestic quadrotors).</p> 
				 	]]>
				</content:encoded>							</item>
