					<item>
				<guid>http://www.scpwiki.com/forum/t-9819650#post-4107469</guid>
				<title>+1</title>
				<link>http://www.scpwiki.com/forum/t-9819650/scp-1322-j#post-4107469</link>
				<description></description>
				<pubDate>Thu, 03 Jan 2019 20:23:54 +0000</pubDate>
								<wikidot:authorUserId>4980602</wikidot:authorUserId>				<content:encoded>
					<![CDATA[
						 <blockquote> <p>Success: N</p> </blockquote> <p>lol oh my gosh.</p> <p>I guess I'm a sucker for really straight-faced, highly-clinical descriptions of really mundane things. But this is more than that; it's a commentary on how logical routines established in everyday life with <span style="white-space: pre-wrap;">____</span> (anomalies in this case) can make it seem like someone is an idiot, when really, they're just stuck in their schema of repetitive thinking. (Considering <em>just</em> the sensory data, you can make an argument that the proposition of a chiral alternate reality is on equal footing to an indestructible mirror. Love it.)</p> <p>My only issue is that, while it clearly isn't against the rules of the contest, I would have thought a -J kinda defeats the purpose of making a good article out of what would otherwise be a community pitfall; don’t -J's absolve those pitfalls by their nature?</p> <p><strong>Edit:</strong> My favorite part is when they strap <em>a second</em> drone on top to see if that does the trick.</p> 
				 	]]>
				</content:encoded>							</item>
